  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Reader Q&A: After Lula’s sensational victory in Brazil’s presidential election, post your questions for the Guardian’s Latin America correspondent",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/oct/27/brazil-election-reader-questions-lula-bolsonaro",
      "description": "<p>Having lost the first round of Brazil’s presidential election to far right rival Flávio Bolsonaro, incumbent Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva spectacularly triumphed in the second. What comes next for Brazil? The Guardian’s Tom Phillips will be answering your questions from 15:00 GMT today.</p><p><strong>Ask a question: log in or sign up </strong><a href=\"https://profile.theguardian.com/signin\"><strong>here</strong></a><strong> to comment, or email conversations@theguardian.com</strong></p><p>After losing the first round of Brazil’s presidential election to far right rival Flávio Bolsonaro, incumbent Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, better known simply as Lula, pulled off the most stunning political fightback in modern Brazilian history by winning the second, in so doing securing a fourth presidential term. It will see him become the country’s longest-serving leader.</p><p>The result – 51.79% to Lula, 48.21% to Bolsonaro – was delivered after a supreme court judge had extended polling time by two hours, and after Lula’s camp accused their opponents of sabotaging public transport in an attempt to keep their voters from the polls. It was the first time in Brazilian electoral history that a first-round verdict had been overturned.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/oct/27/brazil-election-reader-questions-lula-bolsonaro\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-27T00:00:03"
    },
    {
      "title": "Brazil: defeated Flávio Bolsonaro ordered to surrender passports",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/26/flavio-bolsonaro-brazil-lula-election",
      "description": "<p>Far-right senator banned from leaving country as supreme court judge’s ruling makes reference to money-laundering case</p><p>The far-right senator Flávio Bolsonaro was banned from leaving Brazil and ordered to surrender his passports just hours after his dramatic loss in the presidential election to the leftist president Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva.</p><p>The ruling was issued by supreme court justice Alexandre de Moraes, the same justice who oversaw the trial of the senator’s father, the former president Jair Bolsonaro, for attempting a coup.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/26/flavio-bolsonaro-brazil-lula-election\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-26T23:02:17"
    },
    {
      "title": "How the streets and social media drove Lula to a historic comeback in Brazil",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/26/lula-victory-brazil-bolsonaro-analysis",
      "description": "<p>A viral WhatsApp channel and student-led protests played a key role in the veteran Brazilian leftist’s election victory</p><p>After the first round of the Brazilian presidential election three weeks ago, the leftist incumbent Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva <a href=\"https://oglobo.globo.com/politica/eleicoes-2026/especial/a-ultima-danca-por-uma-virada-inedita-surpreendido-no-primeiro-turno-lula-ressurge-aprendendo-com-as-ruas-e-as-redes.ghtml\">reportedly</a> told allies he could not understand how he finished second after a government he considered the best of his three terms.</p><p>A mood of dismay swept Brazil’s left, and because 9 million voters for the other candidates, all of them on the right, were expected to mostly transfer to senator Flávio Bolsonaro, analysts widely saw the far-right senator’s victory as certain.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/26/lula-victory-brazil-bolsonaro-analysis\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-10-26T17:56:50"
    },
